<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Guningtou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Kuningtou · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Guningtou was fought on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinmen">Kinmen</a> island, off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> coast, from 25 to 27 October 1949. Nationalist forces destroyed a Communist landing force of about 9,000 men, most of whom were killed or captured. The victory halted the Communist advance across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan-strait">Taiwan Strait</a> and helped the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> keep Kinmen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-guningtou.jpg" alt="Battle of Guningtou" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIA (base map); derivative work by Ran · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ROC_Quemoy.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> was proclaimed on 1 October 1949, the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> withdrew its forces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> but kept garrisons on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinmen">Kinmen</a> and the Matsu islands off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> saw as stepping stones to Taiwan. The PLA 10th Corps under Ye Fei, part of Chen Yi's 3rd Field Army, had no experience of naval warfare and no air or naval cover. Ye Fei chose to take Kinmen and Xiamen at once; Xiamen fell on 17 October, and the attack on Greater Kinmen was given to the 28th Army with regiments of the 29th.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinmen">Kinmen</a> had had no defences before mid-June 1949, when a fortress headquarters began building fortifications, chiefly along the flat western part and the sandy northern shore. Hu Lien's 12th Army Corps was still on its way by sea when the attack came.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At about midnight on 25 October a Nationalist sentry accidentally set off a landmine, and the alerted defenders' searchlights exposed an advancing landing party. Near Lungkou three M5A1 Stuart tanks, one of which had broken down on the beach the evening before, opened fire on the landing craft; the tanks, later nicknamed the Bear of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinmen">Kinmen</a>, were said to have crushed attackers when their ammunition ran out. A second wave early on 26 October was scattered by wind and heavy seas, and only ten platoons landed, lit up by flares dropped from C-47 aircraft.</p><p>By the early morning of 27 October the PLA troops had no food or ammunition left. Over 1,300 were trapped on the beaches below the cliffs north of Kuningtou; after a four-hour assault more than 400 were dead and about 900 had surrendered by 10:00. The commander of the 253rd Regiment, Hsu Po, hid in a cave near Mount Taiwu until his capture in February 1950.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Ye Fei apologised to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, blaming a shortage of landing craft, the failure to secure beachheads and the lack of unified command, but was not punished. Nationalist records give 1,267 of their own killed and 1,982 wounded, and 7,364 PLA soldiers and civilian workers captured; many prisoners were later drafted into the Nationalist army, and about 900 were returned to the mainland in 1950. Hu Lien then fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinmen">Kinmen</a> and developed its roads, forests, schools and Kaoliang liquor production. The Kuningtou Battle Museum opened in 1984, and in May 2025 the Legislative Yuan made 25 October a national holiday.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Guningtou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On Taiwan the armed forces held Kinmen at the <b>Battle of Guningtou</b> in October 1949 and through the Taiwan Strait crises of 1954–55 and 1958, backed from 1954 by a mutual defence treaty with the United States.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Military</a></li></ul></section>
